const body = `<h1>8.3b &#8212; Tic-Tac-Toe (Build Together)</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Basic Application. The teacher builds, you follow along.
    A 2D array as a game board &#8212; print it, place moves, and detect wins.
    Every grid-based game starts with this skeleton.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Build a two-player Tic-Tac-Toe game using a 3&#215;3
    <code>char[][]</code> board. Players alternate placing X and O until
    someone wins or the board is full.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">  0 1 2
0 [ ][ ][ ]
1 [ ][ ][ ]
2 [ ][ ][ ]

Player X: enter row col > 1 1

  0 1 2
0 [ ][ ][ ]
1 [ ][X][ ]
2 [ ][ ][ ]</span></code></pre>

    <p><strong>Reference:</strong>
    <a href="../docs/unit-08/tic-tac-toe.html">Tic-Tac-Toe Walkthrough</a>
    &#8212; full reference.</p>

    <h3>Iteration 1: Create the Board</h3>

    <p>Create <code>TicTacToe.java</code>:</p>

    <pre><code>public class TicTacToe {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char[][] board = new char[3][3];

        // Initialize with spaces
        for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {
                board[r][c] = ' ';
            }
        }

        printBoard(board);
    }

    public static void printBoard(char[][] board) {
        System.out.println("  0 1 2");
        for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
            System.out.print(r + " ");
            for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++) {
                System.out.print("[" + board[r][c] + "]");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why use <code>char[][]</code> instead of
                <code>String[][]</code>? What's the difference?</li>
            <li>What would the board look like if you forgot to fill
                it with spaces? Try it.</li>
            <li>Why is <code>printBoard</code> static? What would need
                to change to make it non-static?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3>Iteration 2: Place Moves</h3>

    <p>Add Scanner and let two players alternate:</p>

    <pre><code>Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
char currentPlayer = 'X';

while (true) {
    System.out.print("Player " + currentPlayer + ": enter row col > ");
    int row = input.nextInt();
    int col = input.nextInt();

    board[row][col] = currentPlayer;
    printBoard(board);

    // Switch player
    if (currentPlayer == 'X') {
        currentPlayer = 'O';
    } else {
        currentPlayer = 'X';
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <p>What happens if a player types "1 1" on a square that
        already has an X or O? It overwrites it! Add a check:
        only place the move if the square is still empty. Also:
        what if they type "5 5"? Add bounds checking.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Iteration 3: Win Detection (The Hard Part)</h3>

    <p>You need to check 8 possible wins: 3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals.
    DO NOT write 8 separate if-statements. Use loops:</p>

    <pre><code>public static boolean checkWin(char[][] board, char player) {
    // Check rows
    for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
        if (board[r][0] == player &&
            board[r][1] == player &&
            board[r][2] == player) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    // YOUR CODE: check columns (same idea, swap r and c)
    // YOUR CODE: check two diagonals
    return false;
}</code></pre>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker Challenge</h3>
        <p>The win check above works but doesn't scale &#8212; what if
        you wanted a 5&#215;5 board? Rewrite the row check using a loop
        that checks every cell in the row without hardcoding each
        column index. (Hint: you'll need a boolean flag inside the
        outer loop.)</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is the grid-based Tic-Tac-Toe board the perfect
                model for your RPG's TileMap? What's the same?</li>
            <li>How would you adapt this board for a Connect Four
                game (6 rows, 7 columns, pieces drop to the bottom)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

